<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.11.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01439</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.11.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>72267119-479e-46de-b986-cd57dfafe469</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para><emphasis><emphasis>Students will complete the following questions to practice the skills they have learned in this lesson.</emphasis></emphasis></para>
<para>For questions 1 – 4, use the following:</para>
<blockquote>
     <para>Function \(h\) describes the height of a ball, in inches, after \(n\)<emphasis> </emphasis>bounces and is defined by the equation \(h(n)=120 \cdot (\frac{4}{5})^n\).</para>
</blockquote>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>
    What is \(h(3)\)? Round to the nearest integer. Think about what it represents in this situation.</item>
</list>
<para>
  <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 61
</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>
    Could \(h(n)\) be 150? 
  </item>
</list>
<para>
  <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>No
</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>
    Which ball loses its height more quickly: This ball, modeled by \(h(n)=120 \cdot (\frac{4}{5})^n\), or a tennis ball whose height in inches after \(n\) bounces is modeled by the function \(f\) where \(f(n)=50 \cdot (\frac{5}{9})^n\)?</item>
</list>
<para>
  <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> The ball modeled by \(f(n)\)
</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>
    How many bounces would it take before the ball bounces less than 12 inches from the surface? </item>
</list>
<para>
  <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 11

</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>
    After a second bounce, a ball reached a height of 70 cm. The rebound factor for the ball was 0.8. From approximately what height, in cm, was the ball dropped?  </item>
  </list>
    <list>
      <item>
        560
      </item>
      <item>
        56
      </item>
      <item>
        21
      </item>
      <item>
        88

      </item>
    </list>

<para>
  <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>d. 88

</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item>Using the table below, find the height, in cm, from which the ball was dropped.

    <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
      <thead/>
      <tbody>
        <row>
          <entry><emphasis>
    Number of Bounces</emphasis></entry><entry>
           1
          </entry>
          <entry>
           2
          </entry>
          <entry>
            3
          </entry>
          <entry>
          4
          </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
            <entry><emphasis>Height in cm</emphasis></entry><entry>
           90
          </entry>
          <entry>
           54
          </entry>
          <entry>
            32
          </entry>
          <entry>
            19
          </entry>
        </row>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </item>
</list>
<para>
  <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 150
</para></section>
</content>
